  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute uncomplicated cystitis (N30.00)</content>. Typical symptoms with a positive dipstick and no fever or flank pain, so this is cystitis, not pyelonephritis. Diabetes raises the risk of urinary infection, and a culture is sent because she has diabetes; with good glucose control and no complicating factor it is treated as uncomplicated. [S]<list><item>Nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily with food for five days.</item><item>Urine culture sent; the result will be phoned.</item><item>Plenty of fluids.</item><item>Return at once for fever, flank pain, vomiting, or no improvement in 48 hours.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Type 2 diabetes during an infection (E11.65)</content>. Home glucose 110 to 140 mg/dL; eating and drinking normally. [S]<list><item>Continue metformin; pause it if she vomits or cannot keep fluids down, and call.</item><item>Delay the semaglutide step up to 0.5 mg by one week, to the preventive visit, so that new nausea is not confused with illness.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2026 (Section 2, Diagnosis and Classification of Diabetes; Section 9, Pharmacologic Approaches to Glycemic Treatment) (2026); International clinical practice guidelines for the treatment of acute uncomplicated cystitis and pyelonephritis in women: A 2010 update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America and the European Society for Microbiology and Infectious Diseases (2011)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in one week as booked; return sooner for any warning sign. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-30: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
